<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the bundle's own Info.plist by convention: the Tauri CLI reads
  `<tauri dir>/Info.plist` if it is there and merges it over the generated one,
  so no line in `tauri.conf.json` points at this file and none should. The
  place to re-check after a CLI upgrade is
  `crates/tauri-cli/src/interface/rust.rs` in the Tauri repository, where
  `tauri_dir.join("Info.plist")` is pushed onto the list handed to
  `merge_plist`; checked against @tauri-apps/cli 2.11.4.

  What it carries is the sentence macOS shows when it asks for one of the
  protected folders. Without one the prompt names no reason at all, which is
  the worst moment to say nothing: a refusal is stored, and the app cannot ask
  a second time. Each sentence says what the folder is wanted for — the project
  somebody opened — rather than what the app is.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Smetana reads and edits the files of the project you open in it — including one you keep on your Desktop.</string>
	<key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Smetana reads and edits the files of the project you open in it — including one you keep in your Documents folder.</string>
	<key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Smetana reads and edits the files of the project you open in it — including one you keep in your Downloads folder.</string>
	<key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Smetana reads and edits the files of the project you open in it — including one you keep on a removable volume.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
